\details{
\subsection{Chain-class purity asymmetry}{

D-segment chains (IGH, TRB, TRD) have hyper-variable CDR3s that uniquely identify
a clone, so substring/exact CDR3 matches against the paired pool are typically pure
(purity = 1.0). Non-D chains (IGK, IGL, TRA, TRG) have constrained CDR3 length and
a limited V repertoire, so the same CDR3 typically appears across many unrelated
paired clones, producing low-purity, untrustworthy rescues.

The default \code{purity} argument therefore encodes this asymmetry: D-gene chains rescue
at 0.9, non-D chains skip rescue entirely (\code{NA}). Users can override per-chain.
}

\subsection{Outputs}{

On a sticky run the function adds these columns to the Seurat metadata:
\itemize{
\item \code{Res_clonality} — the (possibly rescued) clonality ID
\item \code{Res_clonality.correction} — \code{"Normal"}, \code{"Corrected"}, \code{"Not tested"},
\code{"Not tested (chain skipped)"}, \code{"Not tested (cdr3 too short)"}, or
\code{"Not tested (no match)"}
\item \code{Res_dominant_purity} — numeric in [0, 1] giving the dominant-paired-clone fraction
among all paired pool rows that matched this single's CDR3, or \code{NA} if not tested.
Useful for plotting (e.g. alluvial of single-clone → paired-clone with ribbon
alpha proportional to purity).
\item \code{Res_mapping_source} — which CDR3 column produced the match
}
